The Sri Yantra is built from nine interlocking triangles, four pointing up and
five pointing down, arranged around a single central point. From that
intersection radiate forty-three smaller triangles, then lotus petals, then a
square boundary with gates at the four directions. It is one of the most
analyzed figures in all of sacred geometry.

## A diagram of creation

In the tradition, the central point is the origin, the seed from which the
cosmos unfolds. The triangles represent the interplay of complementary forces;
their overlap generates the manifest world.

## Why it is hard to draw

Getting all nine triangles to intersect cleanly at the correct points is a
genuine geometric problem. Small errors compound, and a precise Sri Yantra
requires careful construction.

This is a placeholder article. The full research piece is in progress.
